<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wuchang</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wuchang is a historic city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze River</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, now part of the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuhan">Wuhan</a>. On 10 October 1911 a military mutiny there triggered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinhai-revolution">Xinhai Revolution</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a> describe how the uprising spread rapidly as provincial assemblies declared independence, leading to the abdication of the last emperor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puyi">Puyi</a>, in 1912.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wuchang.jpg" alt="Wuchang" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: xiquinhosilva · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yellow_Crane_Tower_61491-Wuhan_(49150476391).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
